13	// They are two kernels here only because they belong to two mixers. Every
14	// layer is mix, block, combine, and then the *next* mixer's norm — and the
15	// combine's output is precisely the norm's input, one stream at a time, with
16	// no other reader in between. Both are already one workgroup per
17	// (token, stream), so the fusion needs no new decomposition: a workgroup
18	// combines its own 2560 values, keeps them in registers, and norms them
19	// without going back to DRAM.
